answers.after:
E5002 at `safe fn clear_pixels(buf: PixelBuf)`. `buf.data` is a raw pointer that C reads and that safe code may have set. The message names it and gives the line to write: `@safety("`buf.data` points at `buf.len` writable bytes")` above `unsafe fn clear_pixels(buf: PixelBuf)`. The safe interface is an ordinary wrapper:

unsafe extern "C":
    @safety("`buf.data` points at `buf.len` writable bytes")
    @ffi(no_callbacks)
    unsafe fn clear_pixels(buf: PixelBuf)

fn clear(ref mut bytes: MutSpan[u8]):
    # SAFETY: the struct describes exactly the bytes of `bytes`
    unsafe:
        clear_pixels(PixelBuf(data=bytes.as_mut_ptr(), len=bytes.len()))

It is called `clear(ref mut frame.as_mut_span())`, so safe callers can clear only bytes they own.

